    </aside><figure class="van-image-figure inline-layout kiosq-b" data-bordeaux-image-check="" id="elk-0055d69b-c733-4f99-86bb-3e57d5b6d613" aria-hidden="true"><div class="image-full-width-wrapper"><div class="image-widthsetter" style="max-width:2400px;"><p class="vanilla-image-block" style="padding-top:56.25%;"> <picture data-new-v2-image="true">
<source type="image/webp" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1920-80.jpg.webp 1920w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1600-80.jpg.webp 1600w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1280-80.jpg.webp 1280w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1024-80.jpg.webp 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-768-80.jpg.webp 768w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-415-80.jpg.webp 415w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-360-80.jpg.webp 360w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-320-80.jpg.webp 320w" sizes="(min-width: 710px) 670px, calc(100vw - 30px)" />
<img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5.jpg" alt="A Hubble photograph showing thousands of stars shining in the SMC" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1920-80.jpg 1920w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1600-80.jpg 1600w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1280-80.jpg 1280w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-1024-80.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-768-80.jpg 768w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-415-80.jpg 415w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-360-80.jpg 360w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5-320-80.jpg 320w" sizes="(min-width: 710px) 670px, calc(100vw - 30px)" loading="lazy" data-new-v2-image="true" data-original-mos="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5.jpg" data-pin-media="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/CNC7Vhk3K3KZzd3WEvCGn5.jpg" class="rounded-[var(--image--border-radius,0)]">
</picture></p></div></div><figcaption class="inline-layout "><div class="caption-text"><p>Researchers tracked the movements of thousands of stars within the SMC in order to make the surprising discovery.</p></div></figcaption><div class="credit">(Image credit: ESA/Hubble)</div></figure><p id="elk-a554858a-eba6-41c2-aa89-765964bef926" aria-hidden="true" class="kiosq-b">At this speed, the stars will have moved by "several thousand light-years" over the next few hundred million years, which will be "enough to significantly distort the galaxy's structure," the researchers said in a <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.aip.de/en/news/smc-disturbed-and-expanding/" target="_blank" data-url="https://www.aip.de/en/news/smc-disturbed-and-expanding/" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link"><u>statement</u></a>. There is, therefore, a decent chance that the SMC will be completely cleaved in half by the time both the SMC and the LMC <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.livescience.com/space/cosmology/catastrophic-collision-between-milky-way-and-andromeda-galaxies-may-not-happen-after-all-new-study-hints" data-url="https://www.livescience.com/space/cosmology/catastrophic-collision-between-milky-way-and-andromeda-galaxies-may-not-happen-after-all-new-study-hints" data-hl-processed="none" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.livescience.com/space/cosmology/catastrophic-collision-between-milky-way-and-andromeda-galaxies-may-not-happen-after-all-new-study-hints"><u>eventually collide with the Milky Way</u></a> in around 2.4 billion years.</p><p aria-hidden="true" class="kiosq-b">This is not the first research to suggest that the LMC is tearing the SMC apart. Last year, a similar study revealed that some of the smaller cloud's stars <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.livescience.com/space/astronomy/astronomers-are-shocked-to-find-our-galaxys-nearest-neighbor-is-being-torn-to-shreds" data-url="https://www.livescience.com/space/astronomy/astronomers-are-shocked-to-find-our-galaxys-nearest-neighbor-is-being-torn-to-shreds" data-hl-processed="none" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.livescience.com/space/astronomy/astronomers-are-shocked-to-find-our-galaxys-nearest-neighbor-is-being-torn-to-shreds"><u>were moving in opposite directions</u></a> from each other. However, the new paper is the first to prove this is happening across the entire dwarf galaxy.</p><script data-id="vanilla-slice-newsletterForm-articleInbodyContent-29dH62h582bBdwUqxxd9JY-hydrate" type="text/javascript">
    window.sliceComponents = window.sliceComponents || {};

    externalsScriptLoaded.then(() => {
